 ],
 "literal": "You indeed, others' guarded valiant kings' fortresses / without sparing, conquering and destroying, their / crowns made of pure gold / to adorn your feet, wearing heroic anklets / O strong warrior, victorious king! / We indeed, those who sing your slander, their necks bowing down, / those who sing your praise, their splendor appearing, / as we see you today, may we see you always! / Be of sweet words and easy of access, O great lord, / of the land where a small space where a single female elephant lies down / supports seven male elephants!",
 "translation": "O victorious king, mighty warrior! You conquer and destroy the well-guarded fortresses of other brave kings without mercy, fashioning anklets for your feet from the pure gold of their crowns. We wish to see you always just as we see you today—with the necks of those who slander you humbled, and the splendor of those who sing your praise shining bright. O great lord of a fertile land where even a tiny patch of ground where a single female elephant lies down can yield enough grain to feed seven male elephants, may you always speak sweet words and remain easy to approach!",
 "modern": "வெற்றி வேந்தனே, வலிமைமிக்க வீரனே! நீ பகை மன்னர்களின் பாதுகாப்பான கோட்டைகளை இரக்கமின்றி அழித்து, அவர்களின் முடிகளில் உள்ள பசும்பொன்னைக் கொண்டு உன் வீரக்கழலைச் செய்து அணிந்து கொள்கிறாய். உன்னைப் பழித்துப் பேசுவோரின் தலைகள் பணியவும், உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பாடுவோரின் முகம் பொலிவு பெறவும், இன்று காண்பது போலவே எந்நாளும் உன்னைக் காண விரும்புகிறோம். ஒரு பெண் யானை படுக்கும் அளவிலான சிறிய நிலப்பகுதியே ஏழு ஆண் யானைகளுக்கு உணவளிக்கும் அளவுக்கு வளம் மிக்க நாட்டின் தலைவனே! நீ எப்போதும் இனிய சொற்களைப் பேசுபவனாகவும், எளியோரும் எளிதில் அணுகக்கூடியவனாகவும் இருப்பாயாக!",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the poet Āvūr Mūlaṅkiḻār addresses the Chola king Kiḷḷivaḷavaṉ, offering him gentle counsel on how to rule wisely. The poet begins by praising the king's immense military power, noting how he melts down the golden crowns of defeated enemies to fashion heroic anklets for his own feet. He expresses a wish to always see the king in this glorious state, where detractors are humbled and loyal singers are elevated. To emphasize the king's responsibility, the poet describes the incredible fertility of the Chola land, where a tiny plot of ground large enough for a single female elephant to lie down can yield enough grain to feed seven male elephants. Finally, he advises the great ruler to remain easily accessible to his people and to always speak to them with sweet, gentle words.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}